  "dom_id": "step-q48053",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=3?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<3 \\\\ 0, & x=3 \\\\ 2x+3, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- $f(3)=0,$ so $f(3)$ exists. $\u2713$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2} \\\\  & =9, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(2x+3) \\\\  & =2(3)+3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=9.\\,\\,\u2713$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=9,$ while $f(3)=0.$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3$ because ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3).$",
